[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The King's Indian is a hypermodern opening, where Black deliberately allows White control of the centre with pawns, with the view to subsequently challenge it.

This opening starts if White plays the move d4. }
1. d4 { Now we don't play d5 and be met with the boring London System, or the aggressive Queen's Gambit.
And also we don't play e5, the tricky Englund Gambit, or f5, the rarely-seen Dutch. Instead, we play Lord Fish's best move, Nf6! } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 1... Nf6! { We take control of the center, developed a piece, and also discourages e4. But e4 will be still played by some Omega Gambit players. } 2. c4 { White takes the center with a flank pawn, and at the same time, controls the center square d5. Now we try to fianchetto the bishop by playing g6! } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 2... g6! { We ignore White of taking the center, instead developing piece actively. Don't be worry about the center, we'll fight it back after that. } 3. Nc3 { White intends to play e4 with this move. Developing this knight first also keeps options open for being able to play multiple lines against the King's Indian. Just don't mind his plan and play Bg7! } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 3... Bg7! { Here White usually plays e4, following his plan. Other moves other than e4, according the Lord Fish, is just a simple 0.0. } 4. e4 { Now to disable White to kick our knight with e5, play d6, which is also the main line. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gd7d6] } 4... d6 { The move d6 ensures that White can't kick the Black Knight with e5. It also prepares to play e5 or c5 at some possible point.
White has a variety of choices here. Let's explore them one by one!🚀 } *

