1. Nf3 { Now let's castle our king here. After that, we can play c5, or e5 to changelle White's center. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 1... O-O { Did you know that this position is part of the Classical King's Indian Defence? } 2. Be2 { Now let's strike back in White's trio pawn center with e5! } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 2... e5 { The move e5 attacks the center and gains space. The main line continues by White castling kingside to get the king away from the center.
Next we'll discuss some sidelines from White, other from O-O. Here's the sideline moves:
🪔 d5, the Petrosian Variation
🪔 And dxe5, the Exchange
Let's start!🛫 } 3. O-O { Now develop a piece by playing the move Nc6! } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 3... Nc6 { White would mostly kick out the knight with d5. } 4. d5 { Which square is the best for the knight? Take a wild guess, if you don't mind. } 4... Ne7 { You found the correct move!🎉Now we have to cover 2 possible responses by White: Ne1 and b4. Let's discover Ne1 first!🛫 } (4... Nb8 { Why would you go back home when you can continue to play at the field? }) (4... Nd4 { This loses a pawn after the combo by White: Nxd4 exd4 Qxd4. }) (4... Nb4 { This move loses tempo by White's improving pawn move a3. }) (4... Na5?? { This simply loses a piece if White plays b4. After b4, the knight is totally trapped on the edge. }) 5. Ne1 { This move tries to reroute the knight to the queenside. Let's do the same thing, then! } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gf6d7] } 5... Nd7 { We hope to place the knight on b6. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd7b6] } 6. Be3 { Now create kingside play by playing f5. Since we have a lot of defenders along the king, we can safely moves the f-pawn. } { [%csl Gf7] } 6... f5 { We hope to open the f-file for our rook - if Black takes the pawn. } 7. f3 { This move is played 99 percent on Lichess alone. Now kick out the DSB with f4! } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf5f4] } 7... f4 { We kick the bishop out of the game. } 8. Bf2 { Now support the f4 pawn! } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg6g5] } 8... g5 { Now we can continue our normal development with moves like Nf6 and Ng6. } *

