5. b4 { Another great choice for White. Now create some queenside attack by playing a5! } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga7a5] } 5... a5 { Now White could play 2 popular moves here: Ba3, protecting the pawn on b4, or bxa5, when they trade of the pawns. Let's explore Ba3 first!🛫 } { [%csl Ga3,Ga5][%cal Gc1a3,Gb4a5] } 6. Ba3 { Let's just simply take the pawn here. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga5b4] } 6... axb4 { White must retake, or else the knight or the bishop would be gone! } 7. Bxb4 { Now play b6, preparing c5, attacking the poor bishop. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb7b6,Gc7c5] } 7... b6 8. a4 { If White plays other moves, please DO NOT play c5 attacking the bishop, because White can capture the pawn by playing the famous en passant. Now play, Ne8. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf6e8] } 8... Ne8 { Preparing Nc7. You might wonder, isn't the c7 square occupied? Yes, but you'll see. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Ge8c7] } 9. a5 { Follow my words here. Kick the bishop out with c5. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc7c5] } 9... c5 { Now the c7 square is cleared! } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Ge8c7] } 10. dxc6 { En passant here is correct, but please remember, don't always en passant when you can. Choose the right moment!
Now please, take back the lost pawn, and at the same time, attacking the bishop. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ge7c6] } 10... Nxc6 { Be prepared for Ba3 and Nd5 here! Good luck fighting with your opponent! } *

