[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. f4 { Now we play, O-O, safening the king. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 1... O-O { We are not concerned of White's attack as the attack is too weak. Pawns can't scare us! } 2. Nf3 { Now play c5, attacking the center! } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc7c5] } 2... c5 { We strike back in the center. } 3. d5 { Now play e6, tending to trade off the pawns, and opening up the position. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 3... e6 { Here White can play either Be2, or Bd3. Let's explore Be2 first!🚀 } 4. Be2 { Developing a piece. Take on d5! } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 { Now centralize the rook with Re8! } { [%csl Be8][%cal Bf8e8] } 5... Re8 { Preparing Nxe4 Nxe4 Rxe4!
Now White might play either e5, or Qc2! We'll start with e5 first. } 6. e5 { Now follow my arrows! Take the pawn. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd6e5] } 6... dxe5 7. fxe5 { Two mighty pawns in the center looks scary, but it actually isn't. Play, Ng4! } 7... Ng4 8. e6 { The horde of pawns march to promote, but it's not so easy. Play fxe5. } 8... fxe6 9. d6 { If 9. dxe6 Qxd1+! ( In-between check ) is the right move. After 10. B/N/Kxd1 Bxe6! , We have more active pieces, and also, we're up a full pawn.
Now simply play Nc6, ignoring the advancing pawn. } 9... Nc6 10. O-O { Now play Bd7, developing the LSB and blocking the pawn. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 10... Bd7 { Now we have a very good position, and we're up a pawn. Prepare for Ne4, the very absurd Kh1! , and Bg5. } *

