4. Bd3 { Developing a piece. Play exd5, opening up the position! } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 { Now play b5! , sacrificing a pawn. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 5... b5! { Both Bxb5, O-O and Nxb5 is possible here. We start with...Bxb5!🪔 } 6. Bxb5 { Now sacrifice...
The KNIGHT!!! } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 6... Nxe4!! 7. Nxe4 { Now we win the piece back in style with...Qa5+, forking the king, and the bishop! } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 7... Qa5+! { A beautiful combination, winning a pawn! } { [%csl Bb5,Be1][%cal Ba5b5,Ba5e1] } 8. Nc3?! { Worse than the absurd Kf2!. Play the only move here, Bxc3+! } 8... Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 { Now take that free bishop on b5! } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Ba5b5] } 9... Qxb5 { Stopping the king from castling kingside. } { [%csl Bf1][%cal Bb5f1] } 10. Kf2! { A smart way of doing a "artificial castling", After Rf2, White can simply play Kg1 and pretend that they castled.
Now we play Bf5! } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 10... Bf5 { Developing a piece. } 11. Re1 { Controlling the open e-file. Let's continue our developmentwith Nd7! } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 11... Nd7 { End of the first chapter of Bd3. Be sure to leave a 💖 if you enjoyed it!😀 } *

