[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/hEPV1nVh/z2wMMgFq"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Chessmate_0204"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.08.04"]
[UTCTime "06:07:52"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. f3 { 🌾This is the Sämisch Variation, a well-known and popular line in the King's Indian.
🌾With this solid move, White bolsters his centre. This can be a tough nut to crack for black, who usually wants white to overextend when playing the King's Indian.
🌾But now let's start cracking this nut with O-O! } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 1... O-O { We safen our king. } 2. Be3 { Now let's strike back in the center with c5! } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc7c5] } 2... c5 3. Nge2 { Now we continue our development with Nc6! } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 3... Nc6 4. d5 { Move the knight here, but move it to where?🧐 } 4... Ne5! { We centralize our knight. } (4... Nb8 { Why let it go back home? He hadn't enjoy the party yet... }) (4... Nb4 { You sure? After a3, the knight will dances around until he feels dizzy. }) (4... Na5 { Congrats, this is the best move! But here let's find the second best move! }) (4... Nd4?! { A blunder of a pawn. Nxd4 cxd4 Bxd4, the c-pawn is lost. }) 5. Ng3 { Rerouting their knight. Now we start an attack on the kingside with h5! } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 5... h5 6. Be2 { Now continue advance Harry the h-pawn! } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh5h4] } 6... h4 { Kicking out the knight, forcing it to go to f1. } 7. Nf1 { Now play, e6! } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7... e6 { We tend to trade of the pawns and open a the f-file for the rook. And this is the end of the Sämisch Variation. Be sure to leave a💖before you go! } *

